WEBVTT

00:00:00.658 --> 00:00:20.002
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. Today Australia banned AI vocals from its music charts after one DJ's fake Madonna cover topped the radio play charts before anyone had a rule against it. Plus, Williams Lake just wrapped eight weeks of live, all-local Performances in the Park — so we're asking who actually gets heard, and who owns the recording. Coming right up.

00:00:33.477 --> 00:00:52.565
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Monday, August 31st, 2026. Here in the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations. Today's theme is Arts, Culture &amp; Digital Storytelling.

00:00:53.287 --> 00:01:02.279
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're looking at galleries, festivals, and music charts today — who gets to make culture out here, and what it costs them to be seen doing it.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:27.660
<v Casey>Quick weather sweep before we get into it. Horsefly Lake is sitting at 9 degrees with overcast skies, high of 21 expected today, low of 9 tonight, and tomorrow looks like more of the same — barely any precipitation showing up in the forecast. Around the region: 100 Mile House at 8, Williams Lake at 10, Quesnel at 10, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Redstone's holding at 7. Overcast pretty much everywhere.

00:01:28.047 --> 00:01:31.847
<v Riley>No rain relief, but at least it's not smoke. Let's get into the news.

00:01:33.247 --> 00:02:01.463
<v Riley>Good stretch of ground to cover today, close to home especially. Williams Lake just closed out eight weeks of Performances in the Park — the free summer concert series in the downtown wrapped this past weekend after two months of live music every week. Local musicians took the stage, volunteers ran sound and setup, no admission charged at any point in the run. The Tribune's photo coverage shows people dancing in front of the stage on the final night.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:08.436
<v Casey>Eight straight weeks, all volunteer-run. That's a commitment most organizations don't sustain past year two.

00:02:09.756 --> 00:02:39.668
<v Casey>Staying with the Cariboo — the Pear Lake wildfire near Clinton got two separate updates from the Tribune over the weekend. Saturday afternoon, the Thompson-Nicola Regional District downgraded more properties from evacuation alert to all clear, after the fire showed minimal growth through the week. Sunday, wildfire service crews were still out working a containment line on the south flank, according to reporter Ty Lim's piece. The fire isn't out, but after a summer of repeated evacuation alerts, that's the kind of news people have been waiting for.

00:02:40.951 --> 00:02:55.743
<v Riley>A searobin — a bottom-dwelling fish with leg-like fins — was filmed walking across the ocean floor in footage ScienceAlert is calling strange. It uses those fins the way a crab uses legs, picking its way across the seabed instead of swimming.

00:02:56.984 --> 00:03:24.720
<v Casey>On the subject of things worth documenting properly — a Los Angeles architecture studio called KFA is set to convert a 1970s brutalist office tower into apartments, after the building recently picked up a historic landmark designation. Dezeen's coverage says the plan takes advantage of an adaptive reuse policy the city adopted recently, letting developers repurpose the concrete shell instead of demolishing it. The interior becomes housing units. The exterior stays intact.

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:28.559
<v Riley>Somebody decided ugly and irreplaceable aren't the same thing.

00:03:30.152 --> 00:03:54.752
<v Riley>One more worth flagging for anyone tracking press freedom precedent. The Pentagon fired the publisher, the editor-in-chief, and a senior reporter at Stars and Stripes after the paper's leadership made public comments defending its editorial independence. The Committee to Protect Journalists called it a direct move against a newsroom that's covered the U.S. military continuously since the 1940s.

00:03:56.103 --> 00:04:17.231
<v Casey>Rounding out the rest of the list. Virginia Tech researchers found a process to turn PVC — one of the most stubborn plastics to recycle — into a key ingredient for high-performance lubricants, the kind used in engine oil. ScienceDaily's writeup says the technique could give a use to mountains of window frames, pipe scraps, and vinyl siding that have had nowhere to go for decades.

00:04:18.436 --> 00:04:38.492
<v Riley>In Gary, Indiana, residents are furious at their utility company after a blackout that dragged on for days across the city and surrounding Northwest Indiana communities. Mother Jones' reporting, done with Capital B, quotes locals calling it corporate neglect rather than bad luck, pointing to years of deferred maintenance on aging infrastructure.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:53.984
<v Casey>TechRadar put the new Ninja CrushBOSS through a full kitchen test — a three-in-one food processor and blender — and their reviewer says it handled everything they threw at it without a hiccup. Somebody in the Cariboo's going to be asking about one for Christmas.

00:04:55.406 --> 00:05:15.278
<v Riley>Business Insider has a piece tracing a line from AI data center construction to a memory chip shortage that's now pushing up prices on phones, laptops, cameras, and game consoles. The data centers are buying up so much memory capacity for AI training that consumer electronics manufacturers are competing for what's left.

00:05:16.684 --> 00:05:27.940
<v Casey>Lifehacker published a walkthrough on actually deleting your information from online dating platforms, since closing your account on most of these services doesn't mean the company stops holding your data.

00:05:29.106 --> 00:05:41.370
<v Riley>And over at Resilience dot org, there's a longer essay asking whether the world is actually becoming multipolar, or whether that's just the theory everyone's agreed to repeat without checking it against anything.

00:05:42.631 --> 00:06:17.135
<v Casey>Last story of the roundup, and it's a good one to end on. Australia had a nationwide phone outage recently that Hackaday is calling an embarrassing failure — not a natural disaster, not a cyberattack, just a software fault in a carrier's core network that took down calling and texting across the entire country at once. Phones used to be one of the most reliable utilities of the twentieth century, running on their own separate infrastructure with their own backup power. Modern networks have folded that redundancy away in the name of efficiency, and this is what happens when the one remaining system has a bad day.

00:06:18.548 --> 00:06:27.196
<v Riley>The more centralized and connected a system gets, the further a single failure travels. We're going to get into that from the music side of things in a minute.

00:06:28.596 --> 00:06:52.956
<v Casey>Back-to-school season is a good nudge to mention Cariboo Chilcotin Partners for Literacy. They run free adult literacy, English language learning, and digital skills programs across the whole Cariboo Chilcotin region — no cost, no judgment, whether you're brushing up on reading, working on English as a second language, or trying to figure out email and online banking for the first time in your sixties. Find them at caribooliteracy dot com.

00:06:53.605 --> 00:06:59.853
<v Riley>Digital literacy programs are exactly the kind of infrastructure nobody notices until the day they need it.

00:07:00.145 --> 00:07:01.393
<v Casey>Now, onto today's Deep Dive.

00:07:02.793 --> 00:07:36.145
<v Riley>Australia changed its music charts this past Friday. ARIA's updated Charts Code of Practice now excludes any track that isn't, in their language, substantially human made. A human has to write the song. A human has to perform lead vocals or the primary instruments. AI can sit in a supporting production role — mixing, mastering, some backing texture — but it can't be the performer. And the AI tools involved have to be authorized and lawful, meaning trained on licensed material, not scraped off an artist's back catalogue without permission.

00:07:36.695 --> 00:07:48.783
<v Casey>And the reason ARIA moved at all is one specific DJ. Josh Fawaz put out a fully AI-generated cover of Madonna's "Like a Prayer," and it topped Australian radio play in the middle of the summer, before anyone had a rule against it.

00:07:49.303 --> 00:08:21.607
<v Riley>Millions of streams before the ban existed in any form. But it's not just Australia acting alone here. The IFPI — the international body representing record labels globally — issued similar principles back in July. The Recording Academy already has a Grammy rule barring fully AI-generated songs from award eligibility, while still allowing AI-assisted work as long as there's meaningful human authorship behind it. Three institutions, three different countries and mandates, landing on nearly the same definition within weeks of each other.

00:08:22.133 --> 00:08:45.685
<v Casey>Landing on the same definition on paper. Billboard is a different story. Major labels — Universal, Sony, Warner — proposed the same substantially human-made standard for U.S. charts. But reporting suggests fully AI-generated tracks are still climbing genre-specific Billboard charts right now, while that proposal sits unresolved. The same song could be banned from an Australian chart and climbing a U.S. one at the same moment.

00:08:46.372 --> 00:08:59.236
<v Riley>That's a rollout lag, not a philosophical disagreement. Big bureaucratic chart systems with decades of legacy processes move slower than a single national body updating one code of practice. Give it a cycle or two.

00:08:59.777 --> 00:09:14.081
<v Casey>Or it never converges, because the U.S. market is big enough that nobody with a hit needs Australia's charts to matter. A patchwork where enforcement depends on which country's chart you're aiming at isn't a transition phase. It's the permanent shape of the thing.

00:09:14.605 --> 00:09:33.869
<v Riley>Which gets at something worth putting plainly. Is the goal here to be self-sufficient — making something entirely your own hands and voice can account for — or is the goal to be well-connected, plugged into whatever chart and platform system actually gets you heard? Right now those two goals pull in opposite directions.

00:09:34.391 --> 00:09:51.183
<v Casey>They were never that close together to start with. A songwriter in Horsefly, writing and recording something entirely with their own hands, still needs Spotify, or terrestrial radio, or a chart listing, to reach anyone outside the valley. Being self-sufficient in production buys you nothing in reach.

00:09:51.801 --> 00:10:16.273
<v Riley>That's precisely why the ARIA rule should matter more to a rural artist than to a major-label act. If charts start requiring provable human authorship, the artist who actually writes and performs their own material has a credential the AI-generated cover doesn't. It rewards the kind of work rural musicians are already doing out of necessity, since most of them don't have the budget for a synthetic vocal pipeline anyway.

00:10:16.929 --> 00:10:38.513
<v Casey>How do you audit that. Vocal cloning is close to undetectable with current tools. "Substantially human-made" sounds clean in a press release. In practice it's an honor system with the occasional spot check — the same problem we ran into back in early August talking about provenance documents for physical artwork. Paper policy doesn't stop a fake from reaching the audience first.

00:10:39.048 --> 00:10:51.896
<v Riley>The Grammy process is closer to an actual mechanism. Labels are asked for songwriting credit documentation as part of eligibility review, not just a public statement after the fact. Slower than viral, but it's a real check.

00:10:52.533 --> 00:11:10.469
<v Casey>A real check that arrives after the fact every time. The DJ had already monetized millions of streams before anyone asked him for documentation. The chart ban protects the chart's reputation going forward. It does nothing for the songwriter whose actual composition got buried under an AI cover of a song they didn't even write.

00:11:11.011 --> 00:11:16.771
<v Riley>The timing problem is real — the rule is a fence built after the horse already left the field.

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:38.038
<v Riley>But look at what Williams Lake just wrapped up this weekend — eight weeks of Performances in the Park, entirely local musicians, entirely live. Nobody's worried about an authorship audit there because there's no algorithm standing between the performer and the person sitting on a blanket twenty feet away. That's about as self-sufficient as culture gets.

00:11:38.636 --> 00:11:51.956
<v Casey>And it reaches exactly as far as the park fence. The Performances series is real, and it's good, and it still doesn't answer whether any of that music exists anywhere once the amps get packed into a truck. Is anyone actually recording it?

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:57.402
<v Riley>Probably a phone video here and there, from someone in the crowd. Not an organized archive.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:15.800
<v Casey>So the fully self-sufficient version of this — live, human, local — has zero distribution unless somebody plugs it into precisely the kind of platform infrastructure we've just spent ten minutes worrying could let a fake through the door. You can't get the reach without the network. The network is also where the fakes get in.

00:12:16.317 --> 00:12:34.493
<v Riley>That's the same shape as the phone outage story. Centralize everything through one system and you get speed and scale. You also get a single failure point — a whole country's calls dropping at once, or one viral fake dominating radio play for a month before anyone with authority can respond.

00:12:34.593 --> 00:12:49.673
<v Riley>The answer isn't retreating from the network. It's building smaller, local-first pieces of infrastructure that still plug into the larger network on your own terms, instead of being wholly dependent on someone else's server and someone else's terms of service.

00:12:50.327 --> 00:13:05.839
<v Casey>What does that look like for a musician in Quesnel. "Own your infrastructure" sounds fine in the abstract until you ask who's hosting the files, who's paying the hosting bill, and who fixes it when the plugin breaks in eighteen months and the volunteer who set it up has moved to Kamloops.

00:13:06.361 --> 00:13:20.737
<v Riley>Bandcamp already lets an artist keep ownership of the master recording and set their own price, no label required. That's a middle position — connected to a platform, but the master file itself belongs to you regardless of what the platform does next.

00:13:21.398 --> 00:13:35.238
<v Casey>Bandcamp still runs on somebody else's servers, and Bandcamp has already changed hands once, in a corporate sale that rattled a lot of independent artists about what terms might change next. That's the same fragility, dressed in friendlier branding.

00:13:35.885 --> 00:13:59.597
<v Riley>Every distribution choice carries some dependency. The real question is which dependency you can tolerate, and which one you can walk away from without losing the work. If you own your master file, and Bandcamp folds or gets bought again tomorrow, the songs still exist on your own drive. That's a real difference from a release that only exists inside a streaming service's exclusive catalogue.

00:14:00.117 --> 00:14:13.565
<v Casey>That distinction holds. Ownership of the master file isn't the same kind of self-sufficiency as ownership of the distribution channel, and people collapse those two things together constantly, in a way that hides where the actual risk sits.

00:14:14.253 --> 00:14:32.517
<v Riley>Same logic applies to visual work, not just music. That KFA story about the brutalist office tower is a version of the same argument. You don't tear the structure down because it's inconvenient to preserve. You find a use for it that keeps the original recognizable while letting it function in the present.

00:14:33.097 --> 00:14:51.529
<v Casey>How does that map onto a gallery out here, though. The Cariboo doesn't have a 1970s office tower to reinvent. What it has is smaller institutions — the local museum, community arts councils — trying to decide whether digitizing their visual collections is preservation, or just another platform dependency wearing a different label.

00:14:52.141 --> 00:15:15.037
<v Riley>Digitizing isn't the same category of risk as depending on a streaming chart's rules. A scanned archive sitting on a hard drive, mirrored to the Internet Archive — which we talked through back in July when the archival cost question came up — is closer to the Bandcamp master-file model. You still hold your own copy even if the hosting arrangement changes underneath you.

00:15:15.692 --> 00:15:31.292
<v Casey>Provided someone actually does the mirroring, keeps the file naming and metadata consistent across years, and checks periodically that the files still open. That's continuous labor, not a single afternoon's upload. Almost nothing out here has a dedicated digital archivist on staff.

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:51.246
<v Riley>No. But that's the case for being well-connected rather than purely self-reliant, not against digitizing at all. One small gallery sharing scanning equipment with, say, Williams Lake First Nation's own cultural programming gets both institutions further than either one buying a scanner and letting it sit in a closet.

00:15:51.896 --> 00:16:12.704
<v Casey>Partnership is the well-connected answer to nearly everything we've talked about today. It's also the answer that needs someone accountable to the relationship past the first grant cycle, and that's usually where these things fail — not at the signing ceremony, but three years in, when the person who built the working relationship takes a different job and nobody replaces that function.

00:16:13.374 --> 00:16:27.422
<v Riley>That's a governance problem, not a technology problem. Scanning equipment costs almost nothing now compared to a decade ago. What breaks is who's responsible for keeping the relationship alive once the initial enthusiasm wears off.

00:16:27.987 --> 00:16:39.531
<v Casey>So we land back at the fork the phone outage story handed us this morning. Centralize, and you're fast but fragile. Stay small and self-sufficient, and you're durable but nobody outside the room ever hears it.

00:16:40.086 --> 00:16:55.174
<v Riley>It doesn't have to be a permanent choice between the two. Performances in the Park stayed fully self-sufficient all summer, and it worked precisely because nobody needed that series to reach beyond Williams Lake. The ambition was local. Local was enough.

00:16:55.811 --> 00:17:17.235
<v Casey>And the moment someone wants that same concert heard in Quesnel, or wants it available next year for someone who missed the season, self-sufficient stops being sufficient. How connected you need to be depends entirely on how far you actually want the work to travel — and almost nobody decides that on purpose. They find out by accident, usually after the footage is already gone.

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:54.740
<v Riley>That's a decision a community could make deliberately instead of by accident. Before next summer's series starts, someone on the organizing committee could ask the plain question: are we filming this for an archive, or are we fine with it existing only in the park, for the people who showed up. Answer that up front, and you know exactly how much network you actually need — which is worth putting to whoever's organizing Performances in the Park next spring, before the season starts. One volunteer with a decent phone and an actual plan beats zero volunteers with a vague hope that somebody's filming it.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:18:02.736
<v Casey>And if you're the one who ends up filming next year's series, send us the link — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We read all of it.

00:18:03.025 --> 00:18:04.433
<v Riley>Have a good rest of your Monday.

00:18:04.956 --> 00:18:08.764
<v Casey>Go plug in an amp somewhere. Or don't. That's kind of the whole point today.